  "production_date": "1653",
  "brief_description": "This is a painting paying homage to diligent housekeeping, domestic harmony and a contented husband and wife. We are in a kitchen, so the woman takes centre stage and she sits bathed in light from the high window. She catches our eye, confident perhaps that we will approve of her housekeeping. The pots and pans are clean and orderly, and she has laid out a lunch of bread, cheese and ham on an immaculate  cloth -- a simple meal, but there is plenty of it. The man puffs on his pipe and enjoys the heat of the fire. His job was not to help in the kitchen, but to provide the money for the household. They are also a patriotic couple: a map of the Netherlands hangs by the chimney breast. However, the scene is not a real one.  Van Brekelenkam is representing an atmospheric moment in an ideal home.",
